<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1532">1532</a>–<a href="/years/1535">1535</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> was the campaign led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> from 1532 to 1535. Pizarro captured the Inca ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> and took advantage of a civil war and epidemics. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> treat it as a foundation of Spanish power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and the source of Andean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-conquest-of-the-inca-empire.jpg" alt="Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Lepiani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Captura_de_Atahualpa_-_Juan_Lepiani_1920s.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When the Spanish reached the borders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> in 1528 it stretched from the Ancomayo, now the Patía River in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maule-river">Maule River</a> in what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, and from the Pacific to the edge of the Amazonian forests. It had grown from about 400,000 square kilometres in 1448 to 1,800,000 in 1528, and scholars estimate its population at 12 to 16 million.</p><p>Many regions kept distinct cultures and were reluctant to belong to the imperial project, and travel on foot or by boat made the empire hard to govern. Some scholars, such as Jared Diamond, think the empire may already have passed its peak. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a>, who ruled it, died after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> introduced by Europeans reached the empire, and a civil war broke out between his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and his brothers were drawn by rumours of a rich kingdom, and in 1529 he obtained permission from the Spanish Monarchy to conquer the land they called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. In 1532 he found it very different from five years earlier, since disease had devastated it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a>, who commanded at least 30,000 well-trained soldiers from the north, had escaped the troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a>, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> and the south. Pizarro's 168 soldiers, with his brothers and Indigenous auxiliaries, captured Atahualpa at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> in 1532. Peoples oppressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a>, among them the Huancas, Chankas, Cañaris and Chachapoyas, joined the Spanish.</p><p>In 1533 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> arrived and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> was executed after ordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a>'s death. Pizarro subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> and installed the seventeen-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> as emperor. In 1535 Pizarro founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, and Almagro left for present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. Historians are unsure that a united empire would have defeated the Spanish, given the mortality from disease and the conquistadors' horses, dogs, metal armour, swords, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and firearms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1536 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> rebelled after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a> took his wife, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> and killed Juan Pizarro, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> general Quizo Yupanqui attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. In 1537 Almagro seized Cuzco, and Manco fled to Vilcabamba, capital of the Neo-Inca State. Hernando Pizarro executed Almagro in 1538, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> was murdered by Almagro's supporters in 1541, and Manco was murdered by them in 1544.</p><p>In 1572 the viceroy Francisco Toledo declared war on the Neo-Inca State; Vilcabamba was sacked, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru">Túpac Amaru</a>, the last Inca emperor, was captured and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>. The conquest led to campaigns into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> and expeditions into the Amazon Basin, and the region became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire</b>, Francisco Pizarro executed Atahualpa in 1533, entered Cusco and installed Manco Inca, a son of Huayna Capac, as a puppet ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
